"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[7404],{1581(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Divide and Conquer","permalink":"/concepts/tags/divide-and-conquer","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":7,"items":[{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-analysis","title":"Analyzing Divide and Conquer","description":"The loops of iterative algorithms can be analyzed by counting iterations, as we did in the first chapter. Recursive algorithms need a different tool: the execution time is expressed in terms of itself applied to smaller sizes, giving a recurrence equation. Fortunately, most divide and conquer algorithms follow one of two very regular patterns, and for both of them the solution is known in general. This lesson presents those two patterns, explains where their formulas come from, and shows how to apply them.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-analysis"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer","title":"Divide and Conquer","description":"Divide and conquer is one of the oldest and most powerful strategies for designing algorithms. Instead of attacking a problem as a whole, it breaks it into smaller problems of the same kind, solves them (usually by applying the same strategy again), and combines their solutions. We have already met two examples: binary search, which discards half of the array at each step, and Quicksort, which partitions the array and sorts each part.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-examples-2","title":"Divide and Conquer: Classic Problems","description":"This lesson presents some of the most famous divide and conquer algorithms. Two of them, Mergesort and Quicksort, we already know from the sorting chapter; now we can analyze them with the formulas of this chapter. The others (the Towers of Hanoi, the multiplication of large integers, the tennis tournament and the Tromino puzzle) show how the paradigm solves problems that would be hard to approach in any other way.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-examples-2"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-statistics","title":"Divide and Conquer: Majority, Mode and Median","description":"The last group of problems of this chapter comes from statistics this lesson develops the median completely, and leaves the divide and conquer versions of the majority element and the mode as challenges, with hints.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-statistics"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-examples-1","title":"Divide and Conquer: Numbers and Searches","description":"This lesson applies the paradigm and its analysis to a first set of classic problems: the factorial, sequential and binary search, the Fibonacci numbers and the greatest common divisor. Some of them show the strength of divide and conquer; others, especially Fibonacci, show what happens when it is applied badly. All of them are implemented, with tests, in the course repository.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-examples-1"},{"id":"fundamental-problem-domains/quicksort","title":"Quicksort","description":"Quicksort, invented by Tony Hoare around 1960, is one of the most important algorithms ever designed. It is the basis of the sorting routines of many programming languages and libraries, and it is usually the fastest general-purpose sorting algorithm in practice. It improves the idea of exchange: instead of exchanging adjacent elements, it exchanges elements that are far apart, removing many inversions at once.","permalink":"/concepts/fundamental-problem-domains/quicksort"},{"id":"advanced-execution-models/fork-join-framework","title":"The Fork/Join Framework","description":"Creating and coordinating threads by hand is laborious and error-prone. Since Java 7, the package java.util.concurrent includes the Fork/Join framework, designed precisely to execute divide and conquer algorithms in parallel, using all the available processors automatically. This lesson explains how it works and how to use it, with the examples of the course repository.","permalink":"/concepts/advanced-execution-models/fork-join-framework"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);